 The East Coast used to have its own version of Sand Hill Road, but it's about to be rezoned out of existence.

 Catch up quick: The area was nicknamed Mount Money, a collection of office parks perched above a reservoir in Waltham, Massachusetts. Just off of Route 128.

 It was the home of VC firms like Greylock, CRV, Battery Ventures, Matrix Partners, North Bridge Venture Partners, and many others. If you were a Boston-area founder raising money, it was your first — and maybe only — stop. Fast forward: Firms began leaving over a decade ago.

 Some relocated to Boston, mirroring the behavior of Silicon Valley firms that opened San Francisco offices. Some failed. Some essentially switched coasts entirely. Today, there's seemingly no venture left in the Waltham office parks, although there are a small number of PE firms like Webster Equity Partners and Windjammer Capital. State of play: Earlier this month, Waltham's City Council overrode a mayoral veto to allow new mixed-use development at one of the office parks, called Bay Colony Center. 

 Bay Colony is relatively vacant right now, judging by a visit I made yesterday. One entire brick-and-glass building sits empty, while others are clearly below capacity. Parking garages — including one with the "largest garage-mounted solar canopy in the U.S." — are fairly barren. An exception was a building whose tenants include defense-tech giant Anduril, which uses the space for both offices and production. Given the City Council vote, the former venture capital hub is likely to become home to new apartments and slop bowl chains. The bottom line: Mount Money has met its maker.

 
 
